The \OnxCodeTerm{Pair} guard only accepts a positive first field when the second is true. The following \OnxCodeTerm{Pair} arm is needed for the other cases. A safe payload load must be dominated by the corresponding tag test in canonical HIR. This gives independent verification a concrete check even when the source pattern has been erased. Test a case entering each \OnxCodeTerm{Pair} arm and one entering \OnxCodeTerm{Empty}. A missing complement should be diagnosed when the profile requires exhaustive coverage.

\section{Give each test a reason to fail}
Use four inputs for the scoring function. A pair containing 21 and true
gets as far as the guard, passes it and yields 42. A pair containing
21 and false fails the structural test before the guard is reached and
yields 21 from the next arm. A pair containing minus three and true
passes the structural test, fails the guard and yields minus three.
The empty constructor goes straight past both pair arms and yields zero.
The second and third inputs therefore execute the same result expression
for different reasons. Counting only which result expressions were reached
would miss one of the decisions this program makes.

The final match in the file examines a record in a different way.
It asks about the field named x and allows additional fields. Either
of the two listed integer literals can satisfy the field pattern;
the alias names the value that actually matched. With x equal to four
the answer is four, not the ordinal position of the successful
alternative. Replace four in the input with five and the final catch-all
arm supplies minus one instead. If the alternatives introduce bindings,
they must offer the same names with the same types: there is a single
arm body after the choice, and its local names must be valid whichever
alternative led there.

